imgsensor: enumerate the sensor while the privacy latch is engaged

Depends on 0015, which adds gating_current_state().

0015 stops the driver powering the sensor into a rail the privacy latch is
holding down, and makes an open fail cleanly instead. That is enough when
the latch is engaged and cleared within one boot. It is not enough when
the latch is engaged across a reboot, which is exactly what issue #4
describes, because by then the damage is done in userspace rather than in
the kernel.

cameraserver runs the sensor search once, early in boot.
adopt_CAMERA_HW_CheckIsAlive() powers the sensor, reads its chip ID over
i2c and reports whether anything answered. With the latch engaged nothing
can answer, so the search fails, the HAL reports no cameras, and
cameraserver caches that empty device list for the life of the process.
The legacy passthrough provider computes its device list once and has no
cameraDeviceStatusChange path, so nothing can add the camera later.
Clearing the latch afterwards has no effect: the kernel is willing, but no
client ever asks it for anything. Measured on crown, on a boot with the
latch engaged:

    Number of camera devices: 0

A shuttered camera is not an absent camera. It is fitted, it is known, and
it will work the instant the user presses the button. So when the power-on
fails specifically because the latch is engaged, report the configured
sensor as present but unprobed rather than reporting nothing. Opens still
fail while the latch is engaged - kdCISModulePowerOn refuses them, and
0015 makes that refusal cheap and loud - and they start succeeding as soon
as it clears.

This is deliberately narrow. It triggers only when gating_current_state()
says GATED; a genuinely absent or broken sensor still enumerates as absent,
because that is the truth and faking it would hide real faults.

Verified on crown:

  - normal boot, latch clear: "Number of camera devices: 1", camera opens,
    no behaviour change and no latch messages in the log
  - boot with the latch engaged: "Number of camera devices: 1", opening the
    camera is refused with a blank preview, and the refusal does not loop -
    2 log lines over 15s, device fully responsive (the runaway retry that
    patch 0013 exists to prevent does not occur)
  - latch then cleared with a short press: the camera works again with the
    same cameraserver process (pid unchanged), no restart, no reboot and
    no unplug

Apply from the kernel tree root (kernel/amazon/mt8163-4.9):
    patch -p1 < 0016-camera-enumerate-the-sensor-while-the-privacy-latch-is-engaged.patch

diff --git a/drivers/misc/mediatek/imgsensor/src/mt8163/kd_sensorlist.c b/drivers/misc/mediatek/imgsensor/src/mt8163/kd_sensorlist.c
index ae630894..37a9950d 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/mediatek/imgsensor/src/mt8163/kd_sensorlist.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/mediatek/imgsensor/src/mt8163/kd_sensorlist.c
@@ -61,6 +61,7 @@
 /*K.S. kernel standard*/
 #if !defined(CONFIG_MTK_LEGACY)
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
+#include <misc/gating.h>
 #endif /* !defined(CONFIG_MTK_LEGACY) */
 
 /* Camera information */
@@ -1446,6 +1447,34 @@ static inline int adopt_CAMERA_HW_CheckIsAlive(void)
 	/* Bypass redundant search operation of getting sensor ID, */
 	/* if power on failed */
 	if (err != ERROR_NONE) {
+		/* A sensor behind an engaged privacy latch has no power and
+		 * cannot answer on i2c, but it is fitted and it will work the
+		 * moment the user clears the shutter. Reporting "no sensor"
+		 * here is what makes that unrecoverable: cameraserver runs
+		 * this search once at boot and caches the resulting empty
+		 * device list for the life of the process, and the legacy
+		 * passthrough provider has no way to add a camera later. The
+		 * camera then stays missing after the latch clears, until
+		 * something restarts cameraserver.
+		 *
+		 * Report it present but unprobed instead. Opens still fail
+		 * while the latch is engaged - kdCISModulePowerOn refuses
+		 * them - and start working as soon as it is cleared, with no
+		 * restart and no reboot.
+		 */
+		if (gating_current_state() == GATED) {
+			PK_INFO("privacy latch engaged: reporting the sensor as present but unprobed\n");
+			memset(mtk_ccm_name_tmp, 0, camera_info_size);
+			snprintf(mtk_ccm_name_tmp, camera_info_size,
+				 "%s CAM[%d]:%s;", mtk_ccm_name,
+				 g_invokeSocketIdx[KDIMGSENSOR_INVOKE_DRIVER_0],
+				 g_invokeSensorNameStr[
+					KDIMGSENSOR_INVOKE_DRIVER_0]);
+			memcpy(mtk_ccm_name, mtk_ccm_name_tmp,
+			       camera_info_size);
+			g_IsSearchSensor = 0;
+			return ERROR_NONE;
+		}
 		PK_ERR("%s\n",
 			err ==
 			-ENODEV ? "No device in this socket position" :
